WEBVTT

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:03.580
There exists a law which states that

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.670
it is a crime to use anything other than
Bacardi branded rum in Bacardi Cocktails.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.130
"Good luck, old man."

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.550
"Good luck," the old man said.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.470
He fitted the rope lashings of
the oars onto the thole pins and,

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:49.770
leaning forward against
the thrust of the blades in the water,

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:55.110
he began to row out of
the harbour in the dark.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.950
There were other boats from
the other beaches going out to sea.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:06.080
The old man knew he was going far out

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.500
and he left the smell of the land behind

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:15.050
and rowed out into the clean early
morning smell of the ocean.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.630
Ernest Hemingway's
"The Old Man and The Sea".

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.310
Through an elderly fisherman and
the vast sea, he solemnly describes

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.720
both living and dying.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.480
Victory and defeat.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.730
And that which is celebrated through
that struggle...

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.690
human courage.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.740
This evening, your bartender,
Ryu Sasakura,

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.160
will be serving you
the cocktail most loved

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.620
by the writer Ernest Hemingway,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.120
the Frozen Daipuiri.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:55.800
These are the strange beginnings
of bar Eden hall.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:03.010
You're kidding!

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.760
The contract was dead in the water.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.770
No way. I couldn't deal with that.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.100
So damn scary.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.230
You're drunk!

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.610
That's why... so that's why.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.440
That's why I was chosen. I knew it.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.070
What's up with you?

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:23.450
You drunk already?

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.660
No, of course not...

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.540
We'll get another then.
What are you having?

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:30.960
Okay...

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.380
I'll go for...
It's a drink I had a long time ago.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.210
It's a bit like a slush puppy.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.090
Certainly. You mean
a frozen cocktail, don't you?

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.510
It has been getting warmer recently.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:42.720
Good choice.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.760
Hey, hey. Don't go ordering
a kid's drink, you sissy!

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:47.560
Huh?

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.390
You see couples who know
nothing about bars

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.890
ordering those drinks with two straws
in them so they can be all lovey-dovey.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:58.480
Sorry, I'll get something else,
in that case.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.740
Something else? Sheesh...

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:05.200
It's that indecisive, effeminate attitude
of yours that's the problem.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:06.740
That's why...

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.370
Is that why I was chosen?

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.290
Because you knew that...

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.960
I wouldn't have the guts
to refuse the transfer order.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.760
ORDER

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.600
An order...

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:30.430
Well? What is it Shibata?

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.680
Do you accept, or do you refuse?
Let's hear it!

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:35.730
If...

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:36.190
ORDER

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:39.730
ORDER
If I... If I accept this...

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:40.690
ORDER

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.950
Well? What's your answer?

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.950
If I accept this, I'll be... I'll be...

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:50.280
A loser!

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.500
A loser.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:55.750
Listen...

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:59.170
this is a great chance for you to study.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.420
I'm doing this out of the goodness
of my heart, you know.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:04.300
Yes, sir.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.010
Now then, two Martinis please.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:10.180
Very good, sir.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.640
Every man should try a Martini at least
once, even if he's no James Bond!

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:20.020
Right. I really am a loser after all...

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.780
You get it, right?

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.740
I was thinking about you, you dummy.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.410
You think I like lecturing you?
I hope you appreciate it!

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.160
-You hear that?
-He's been transferred.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:38.620
I feel bad for him.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.670
You got that? Gratitude is
what you need, boy. Gratitude.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:43.960
Yes, sir.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.050
You can go now, driver.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.850
Who'd be grateful for being
thrown into a branch out in the sticks?

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:57.350
Huh?

00:05:58.350 --> 00:05:59.400
Damn it.

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.940
Hello? I'm looking for my lost property.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:23.040
...the old man always
thought of her as feminine.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:31.510
...and as something that gave or
withheld great favours...

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.390
...and if she did wild or wicked things,

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:42.900
it was because she could not help them.

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:52.320
"The moon affects her
as it does a woman"

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:56.370
he thought.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:12.180
Oh, good, you're back.
You had forgotten this, sir.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.470
I'm very sorry.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:22.520
I would have returned it to you tomorrow,
but I have plans that will keep me away.

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.650
I wasn't sure what to do.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.230
PIease don't worry. I'm the one
who forgot my belongings.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:33.240
Yes, but I was the one who failed
to realise you'd left it behind.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:38.660
Actually, did you know there is a drink
bartenders regret not serving customers?

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:40.660
Regret not serving...?

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:44.420
That's right. A drink customers can enjoy.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.710
A drink to relax with.
One that calms and heals.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:51.670
Serving that drink is part of
a bartender's role.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:58.060
However, I was unfortunately unable to
provide you that enjoyment earlier.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:02.180
When you're out on the town
with a boss like that

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:04.560
even the nicest drink becomes revolting.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:09.730
Well, even the bartender is
left with a bad aftertaste...

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:15.030
So, is that the drink you forgot to serve?

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:18.580
I know. As I'm here now,

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.290
would you mind making me one more drink?

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:22.540
Very well, sir.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:29.630
I'll be shutting the bar, now.
Please take your time.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:30.920
Thank you.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.720
I was wondering though, what did you
think of the conversation you overheard.

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.640
Quite... Well, I did overhear it but...

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.890
it is not our place as bartenders

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.980
to involve ourselves
in such personal affairs.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.310
Don't worry about that.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:50.820
I probably am a loser anyway...

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:55.400
It's been three years
since I came to Tokyo.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:57.700
When I think about it though,

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:01.450
I've always been more of a burden
on society than anything else.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:02.620
ORDER

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.420
If you read it, you'll see we've decided
that you should run our regional branch.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:08.920
Yes, sir.

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:12.670
I say "regional branch" but then
you will be the only employee there.

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:17.760
Which means you'll take care of everything
from sales, to orders and post-processing.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.140
Yes, sir.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:25.350
I suppose it suits you, actually.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:29.440
Being alone means you won't have
to compete with anyone else's figures.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.730
What's wrong?
Was that too close to the bone?

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:37.150
It was exactly as
Dept. Chief Tajima said.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:41.780
I might be feeling quite relieved
about the transfer order I received.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.500
This is a world that discriminates against
others based on sales figures.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:48.620
Exactly right.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:53.090
And in such a world, there I stand,

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:55.710
unable to find the strength
to fend off the competition.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.340
You know it already.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.220
Naturally, work hasn't been going well.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.470
My sales figures are always the lowest.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.100
In which case you've no complaints, right?

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:10.850
That's right. It's as they tell me,
I am just a loser.

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:13.860
I've forgotten how to fight.

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:15.150
No.

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:17.990
It's still too early to think like that.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.030
But I've already accepted the transfer...

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:24.580
Well, how about
the bartender's forgotten drink?

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:32.750
We start with some ice.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:36.380
Add some rum, lime juice and grapefruit.

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:42.300
And lastly, some Maraschino,
a cherry liqueur.

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:46.970
Then blend the ice until
it resembles sherbet.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:58.360
Is that...

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:05.660
Sorry for the wait.
Your Hemingway Daipuiri.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:15.960
...and the old man saw
his tail out of the water.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:18.800
It was higher than a big scythe blade

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:23.970
and a very pale lavender above
the dark blue water.

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:28.890
It raked back and as the fish swam
just below the surface...

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:32.940
...the old man could see his huge bulk

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:42.490
and the purple stripes that banded him.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.280
His dorsal fin was down,

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:49.700
and his huge pectorals were spread wide.

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:57.670
The old man was sweating now
but from something else besides the sun.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:02.050
On each calm placid turn the fish made

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:06.390
he was gaining Iine...

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:09.560
...and he was sure that in two turns more

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:12.890
he could have a chance
to get the harpoon in.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:24.400
"But I must get him close, close, close,"
he thought.

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:32.120
"I mustn't try for the head.
I must get the heart"

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:42.710
"Be calm and strong, old man," he said.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:54.230
Delicious!

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.310
Thank you.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:58.940
There's no sugar in this drink.

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:02.570
It makes the fruit taste even fresher,
and so easy to drink.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:03.490
Indeed.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:08.280
AIso, Hemingway would usually add
twice the amount of rum to it.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:12.240
Right, you just called it
a Hemingway Daipuiri, didn't you?

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:15.660
That's right. It is also affectionately
called the Fapa Daipuiri,

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:18.420
or, the Wild Daipuiri.

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:23.920
So Hemingway himself
used to drink frozen cocktails...?

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:28.220
Not only that, but the one to make
Frozen Daipuiris world famous

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:29.930
might have been Hemingway himself.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.560
Every evening, when he went to the bar
El Floridita, he would order

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:37.980
twelve double Frozen Daipuiris.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:42.270
He filled his canteen with the same amount
so he could drink on the way home.

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:46.490
So, did Hemingway use
a straw like this one, too?

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:47.780
Surely so.

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:53.540
But the reason there are two straws
in frozen cocktails is so that

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:55.620
you have a spare
when one gets clogged up.

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:58.870
Not for couples
to drink at the same time.

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:01.040
That makes sense.

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:02.790
He'd enjoy fishing in the daytime.

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:05.550
And have Frozen Daipuiris
at the bar in the evening.

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:09.010
It was that cheerful, unassuming image
that made Cubans love him so much.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:13.760
They even gave the beloved writer
the nickname "Papa", apparently.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.140
Papa Hemingway.

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:21.480
But it seems it was in fact one of
the most trying times of his life.

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:24.480
"Trying" how?

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:29.320
The writer who fought in WW1 and
became obsessed with hunting in Africa

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:35.700
and bullfighting in Spain,
did not write anything for 10 years.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:37.620
A whole ten years...?

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:41.790
He was an old man...

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:47.710
...who fished alone in
a skiff in the Gulf Stream

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:52.180
and he had gone eighty-four days
without taking a fish.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:57.310
In the first forty days,
a boy had been with him.

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:07.400
It made the boy sad to see the old man
come in each day with his skiff empty...

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:12.160
The sail was patched
with flour sacks and, furled,

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:20.160
it Iooked Iike the flag of permanent defeat.

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.590
It must have been painful for him.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:30.550
What must he have been thinking
to help him through it...

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:32.880
I can't begin to imagine.

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:36.890
Please take a look at this, then.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:38.890
And this is?

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:41.020
An El Floridita coaster.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:44.310
Actually, this too is an item
forgotten by a customer.

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:47.860
He has the protagonist from
Island in the Stream

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:52.150
say this about Frozen Daipuiris.

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:55.950
"He picked up the heavy glass
with a whitened rim

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:58.700
and the ice hardened like snow."

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:03.080
"The clear part below
reminded him of the sea."

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:07.130
"The clear part was exactly
the colour of the shallow

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:13.880
muddy seawater there
when cut by the ship's bow"

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:16.220
A ship sailing the ocean...

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:21.270
Frozen Daipuiris saved Hemingway
from a ten-year slump,

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:23.560
which leads to his penning
<i>The Old Man and the Sea,</i>

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:28.440
and won him the Pulitzer and Nobel prize
and, ultimately, got him his comeback.

00:16:29.110 --> 00:16:30.190
Is that so?

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:33.820
Or so this bartender
would like to believe.

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:49.590
Thank you, bartender.

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:53.420
Telling me about frozen cocktails and
Hemingway's story,

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:58.390
all to help a loser like me
feel better about my transfer.

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:02.270
<i>If you already get it,
just make your mind up already.</i>

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:05.350
But... I still feel pathetic.

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:09.270
<i>Even after you got told that story
about Hemingway...</i>

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:11.110
I know.

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:13.820
The responsibility is mine to bear.

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:18.030
<i>That's right.
You've got no fight!</i>

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:19.910
That's why I am...

00:17:21.330 --> 00:17:23.080
A loser.

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:27.250
I'm a lo...

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.000
No. Man does not lose.

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:35.010
Man does not lose.

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:40.010
In <i>The Old Man and the Sea,</i>
you will find these words.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:43.220
"Man is not made for defeat."

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:45.430
"A man can be destroyed,"

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:48.520
"but not defeated."

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:51.440
"Man will not be defeated..."

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:59.160
When the old man saw him coming,
he knew that this was a shark

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:04.240
that had no fear at all and would
do exactly what he wished.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:09.710
The old man's head was clear and good now

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:13.840
and he was full of resolution...

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:18.430
...but he had little hope.

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:25.810
"It was too good to last," he thought.

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:32.900
He took one look at the great fish
and he watched the shark close in.

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:38.150
It might as well have been a dream.

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:43.620
"I cannot keep him from hitting me."

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:58.090
"But man is not made for defeat," he said.

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:09.430
"A man can be destroyed but not defeated."

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:20.030
"Don't think, old man," he said aloud.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:29.450
"Sail on this course and
take it when it comes."

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:31.920
I see.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:34.710
So I need to keep sailing this course.

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:36.040
<i>What?</i>

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:39.050
Take the shark when it comes.

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:40.920
<i>What d'you say?</i>

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:48.720
That's right. What Hemingway discovered
over those ten long years, was that...

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:52.890
Man won't be defeated!

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:05.240
Right... So you're accepting the transfer?

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:09.990
I am. However, I refuse to become a loser.

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:14.830
Just watch me.
I will fight tooth and nail.

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:17.790
I will fight and return to this office!

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:20.670
Excuse me.

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:32.810
Oh yeah...

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:44.240
Would it be okay if I popped back in
on my return to Tokyo?

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:46.740
Of course, Mr Shibata.

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:50.030
In that case, I'll see you soon.

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:54.750
We will be waiting.

